  "Number": 5,
  "Question": "Positive integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are each less than \\(6.\\) What is the smallest possible value of the following expression? \\[2 \\cdot a - a \\cdot b\\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( -20 \\)",
    "B": "\\( -15 \\)",
    "C": "\\( -10 \\)",
    "D": "\\( 0 \\)",
    "E": "\\( 2 \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The allowed values satisfy \\(1\\le a,b\\le5\\). Factor out \\(a\\) to make the effect of \\(b\\) easier to see:\n\\[\n2a-ab=a(2-b).\n\\]\nSince \\(b\\le5\\), we have \\(2-b\\ge-3\\). Multiplying by the positive number \\(a\\) preserves this inequality. Also, \\(a\\le5\\) gives \\(-3a\\ge-15\\). Therefore every allowed pair satisfies\n\\[\na(2-b)\\ge-3a\\ge-15.\n\\]\nThe allowed choice \\(a=b=5\\) gives \\(2\\cdot5-5\\cdot5=-15\\), so this lower bound is attained. The smallest value is \\(\\boxed{-15}\\), choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
